---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How it works"
  description="How Operate IQ daily puzzles are generated, when they reset, and where streaks are stored."
  path="/how-it-works"
>
  <section class="play-hero">
    <div class="hero-kicker">Rules</div>
    <h1>How it <em>works</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-lead">
      Three browser games, one shared calendar day. Nothing to install, and nothing to sign in for.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section how-grid">
    <article class="panel">
      <p class="panel-kicker">Daily</p>
      <h2>Same puzzle for everyone</h2>
      <p>
        Each puzzle is generated from the UTC date — YYYY-MM-DD. Anyone opening the site on that day gets the same wordsearch, sudoku, and WordGuess. At 00:00 UTC the boards change.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="panel">
      <p class="panel-kicker">Wordsearch</p>
      <h2>Straight lines only</h2>
      <p>
        Words run in eight directions: across, down, and both diagonals, forwards or backwards. In Play, drag or swipe through the letters. Pinch to zoom around your fingers; slide two fingers to pan, like a map.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="panel">
      <p class="panel-kicker">Sudoku</p>
      <h2>Medium 9×9</h2>
      <p>
        Fill every row, column, and 3×3 box with 1–9. Locked cells are the givens. Notes (N) let you pencil candidates. Conflicts highlight in red.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="panel">
      <p class="panel-kicker">WordGuess</p>
      <h2>Three words, twelve guesses</h2>
      <p>
        Each guess is a five-letter word and scores on all three boards. Teal locks a letter in place; amber means it belongs in that word, other slot. After a guess, the closest unsolved board snaps down next to the keys.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="panel">
      <p class="panel-kicker">Streaks</p>
      <h2>Stored on this device</h2>
      <p>
        Completion and streaks live in your browser's local storage. Clearing site data, switching browsers, or skipping a UTC day resets the count. There is no account and no global leaderboard.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
